import "core"
import "core"


# A declaration naming a data type by its BARE NAME, fulfilled by a definition which destructures
  that type's fields.
  The two sides spell the same type differently: the declaration writes @Plain@ and says nothing
  about what it holds, where the definition writes @/Plain(name, other)@ and binds every field. A
  declaration is fulfilled by a definition inside it, so the bare name has to read as the type with
  its declared fields rather than as one whose fields are unknown — a reading which once gave every
  field [TopType], left the declaration unfulfilled, and failed with
  @Undefined part: /nameOf(/this=(/Plain(/name=TopType - /Data/String, ...)))@.
  A CLASS named the same way is a different thing and was never the question: @intlist.ct@ declares
  @:IntList.len@ over a class and has always passed, because a class reference is not a partial
  carrying fields to be widened. So what this holds is the data type, and only a data type whose
  fields a definition reads.
  The field is read back in Catln rather than by the webdocs, which is the other half of why this
  is here: @maybeField.ct@ and @valueDeclFields.ct@ declare fields and never read one, because a
  note's fields are read by the display rather than by the program.
data(Plain(name -> String, other -> String))

:Plain.nameOf -> String
/Plain(name, other).nameOf= name

main{io -> IO} =
  #assert(test= /Plain(name= "a", other= "b").nameOf == "a")
  io
